1
00:00:03,283 --> 00:00:05,183
Every day in Hong Kong,

2
00:00:05,183 --> 00:00:08,350
about four people die of liver cancer.

3
00:00:08,800 --> 00:00:13,483
That’s 1,412 people in 2022 alone.

4
00:00:13,717 --> 00:00:15,633
Here, it’s one of the three deadliest

5
00:00:15,633 --> 00:00:19,483
cancers, alongside lung and colorectal.

6
00:00:19,633 --> 00:00:21,117
And it’s not just us.

7
00:00:21,167 --> 00:00:23,817
Worldwide, it’s the sixth most common

8
00:00:23,817 --> 00:00:25,967
cancer, and the third deadliest.

9
00:00:26,450 --> 00:00:27,633
Here is the thing.

10
00:00:27,700 --> 00:00:29,400
We already have medicines.

11
00:00:29,400 --> 00:00:30,933
Just not good enough.

12
00:00:31,483 --> 00:00:32,950
The standard first treatment

13
00:00:32,950 --> 00:00:35,383
for advanced liver cancer has already

14
00:00:35,383 --> 00:00:38,450
changed once, from Nexavar to a combo,

15
00:00:38,450 --> 00:00:41,667
atezolizumab plus bevacizumab.

16
00:00:41,817 --> 00:00:42,917
But here,

17
00:00:42,917 --> 00:00:45,850
only about three in ten patients are alive

18
00:00:45,850 --> 00:00:47,117
five years on.

19
00:00:47,283 --> 00:00:49,917
The study we follow calls it a huge

20
00:00:49,917 --> 00:00:51,117
unmet need.

21
00:00:51,600 --> 00:00:54,767
So… why not just make a better one?

22
00:00:55,217 --> 00:00:57,517
Because that’s way harder than it sounds.

23
00:00:57,583 --> 00:01:00,717
Like, ten-to-seventeen-years hard.

24
00:01:01,150 --> 00:01:03,850
Start the search the year we were born,

25
00:01:03,850 --> 00:01:06,333
and it might only be ready now.

26
00:01:06,500 --> 00:01:10,483
It can cost almost 2.8 billion US dollars.

27
00:01:10,617 --> 00:01:13,100
And of drugs tested in people,

28
00:01:13,100 --> 00:01:16,550
only about one in ten reaches the market.

29
00:01:16,650 --> 00:01:20,050
To see why, let’s follow one drug’s journey.

30
00:01:20,333 --> 00:01:23,283
Every medicine walks the same road.

31
00:01:23,433 --> 00:01:24,733
Find a target.

32
00:01:24,750 --> 00:01:25,950
Find a hit,

33
00:01:25,950 --> 00:01:28,933
a first molecule that works on it.

34
00:01:29,050 --> 00:01:30,233
Improve it.

35
00:01:30,250 --> 00:01:33,333
Test it in cells, then animals.

36
00:01:33,600 --> 00:01:35,517
Then human trials:

37
00:01:35,517 --> 00:01:38,083
phase one, two, three.

38
00:01:38,083 --> 00:01:39,283
Then approval.

39
00:01:39,300 --> 00:01:40,817
Any step can fail.

40
00:01:40,950 --> 00:01:43,717
Now here’s where it gets interesting.

41
00:01:43,933 --> 00:01:45,650
In 2024,

42
00:01:45,650 --> 00:01:47,750
part of the Nobel Prize in Chemistry

43
00:01:47,750 --> 00:01:50,767
went to Demis Hassabis and John Jumper,

44
00:01:50,767 --> 00:01:52,183
for AlphaFold,

45
00:01:52,183 --> 00:01:54,817
an AI that predicts the shape of proteins.

46
00:01:54,917 --> 00:01:58,033
So can AI walk the first steps faster?

47
00:01:58,117 --> 00:01:59,650
Let’s follow one real case,

48
00:01:59,650 --> 00:02:02,183
where AI tools took on liver cancer.

49
00:02:02,183 --> 00:02:04,967
And part of it starts right here,

50
00:02:04,967 --> 00:02:05,983
in Hong Kong.

51
00:02:22,917 --> 00:02:24,250
Hong Kong first.

52
00:02:24,250 --> 00:02:26,050
In 2011, a team

53
00:02:26,050 --> 00:02:28,433
at the Chinese University of Hong Kong

54
00:02:28,433 --> 00:02:30,633
studied liver tumours from patients

55
00:02:30,633 --> 00:02:33,000
at the Prince of Wales Hospital.

56
00:02:33,083 --> 00:02:35,317
One protein kept showing up raised:

57
00:02:35,317 --> 00:02:38,717
CCRK, also called CDK20.

58
00:02:38,800 --> 00:02:40,850
Fast forward to 2023:

59
00:02:40,850 --> 00:02:42,883
an AI drug-discovery study goes

60
00:02:42,883 --> 00:02:44,467
after the same protein,

61
00:02:44,467 --> 00:02:45,450
with co-authors

62
00:02:45,450 --> 00:02:48,033
at Insilico Medicine’s Hong Kong office

63
00:02:48,033 --> 00:02:49,400
in Science Park.

64
00:02:49,800 --> 00:02:52,067
So our research question is:

65
00:02:52,067 --> 00:02:54,867
how can computer-aided methods help

66
00:02:54,867 --> 00:02:56,450
scientists find

67
00:02:56,450 --> 00:03:00,000
and assess potential cancer drug candidates?

68
00:03:00,183 --> 00:03:03,467
And we will answer it with one case study:

69
00:03:03,467 --> 00:03:05,817
CDK20 in liver cancer.

70
00:03:06,300 --> 00:03:09,350
We set ourselves two objectives.

71
00:03:09,883 --> 00:03:13,100
One: to identify the major computer-aided

72
00:03:13,100 --> 00:03:16,500
methods used in cancer drug discovery.

73
00:03:16,550 --> 00:03:20,367
Two: to evaluate how these methods help

74
00:03:20,367 --> 00:03:23,700
scientists assess potential drug candidates.

75
00:03:23,800 --> 00:03:27,017
So how did we check all this? We read.

76
00:03:27,017 --> 00:03:27,817
A lot.

77
00:03:28,000 --> 00:03:29,867
No lab work of our own:

78
00:03:29,867 --> 00:03:31,683
this is a literature review.

79
00:03:31,900 --> 00:03:33,067
At the centre,

80
00:03:33,067 --> 00:03:34,800
one original study by Ren

81
00:03:34,800 --> 00:03:36,583
and colleagues in Chemical Science,

82
00:03:36,583 --> 00:03:40,550
2023, with its supplementary data.

83
00:03:40,767 --> 00:03:43,850
Around it, the earlier Hong Kong study,

84
00:03:43,850 --> 00:03:45,683
papers behind two AI tools,

85
00:03:45,683 --> 00:03:49,800
a 2023 review, a textbook chapter,

86
00:03:49,800 --> 00:03:52,917
and a 2026 expert Perspective,

87
00:03:52,917 --> 00:03:55,850
which we read only as an abstract.

88
00:03:55,950 --> 00:03:58,650
For every source, we asked:

89
00:03:58,650 --> 00:04:01,683
what kind is it, how much did we read,

90
00:04:01,683 --> 00:04:03,850
and how strong is the evidence?

91
00:04:03,933 --> 00:04:05,367
Who did the work?

92
00:04:05,433 --> 00:04:08,017
Were there controls and repeats?

93
00:04:08,100 --> 00:04:09,850
One thing worth knowing:

94
00:04:09,850 --> 00:04:12,850
most authors of the main study work

95
00:04:12,850 --> 00:04:15,250
for Insilico Medicine,

96
00:04:15,250 --> 00:04:18,017
which built its AI tools.

97
00:04:18,200 --> 00:04:20,017
They say so openly.

98
00:04:20,017 --> 00:04:23,600
Useful evidence, not an independent check.

99
00:04:23,700 --> 00:04:25,700
So here is where we are going.

100
00:04:26,000 --> 00:04:27,667
First, the lock.

101
00:04:28,317 --> 00:04:31,267
Then how AI helped cut a key for it.

102
00:04:31,767 --> 00:04:33,717
Then what happened in the lab.

103
00:04:34,000 --> 00:04:36,583
And finally, the big one:

104
00:04:36,583 --> 00:04:38,917
how much did the computers really help?

105
00:04:39,517 --> 00:04:40,317
Okay.

106
00:04:40,350 --> 00:04:45,250
To get this, picture a lock and a key.

107
00:04:45,417 --> 00:04:47,033
Inside your cells,

108
00:04:47,033 --> 00:04:49,567
proteins do a lot of the work.

109
00:04:49,767 --> 00:04:52,433
Most drugs grab one protein tightly

110
00:04:52,433 --> 00:04:54,683
and change what it does,

111
00:04:54,683 --> 00:04:57,217
slotting into a pocket like a key

112
00:04:57,217 --> 00:04:58,233
into a lock.

113
00:04:58,333 --> 00:05:01,400
Scientists call the lock the target,

114
00:05:01,400 --> 00:05:03,667
and the key a candidate molecule.

115
00:05:03,683 --> 00:05:06,400
A good key fits its own lock tightly,

116
00:05:06,400 --> 00:05:08,517
and opens no one else’s.

117
00:05:08,750 --> 00:05:10,433
That’s called selectivity.

118
00:05:10,533 --> 00:05:12,683
Our lock is CDK20.

119
00:05:12,800 --> 00:05:14,300
It’s an enzyme,

120
00:05:14,300 --> 00:05:16,467
a protein with a chemical job.

121
00:05:16,517 --> 00:05:19,450
Its keyhole is a pocket where it normally

122
00:05:19,450 --> 00:05:22,150
grabs a small molecule called ATP.

123
00:05:22,567 --> 00:05:25,400
Plug that pocket with the right key,

124
00:05:25,400 --> 00:05:27,600
and the enzyme can’t do its job.

125
00:05:27,700 --> 00:05:28,700
Why this lock?

126
00:05:28,700 --> 00:05:30,317
Remember the Hong Kong team.

127
00:05:30,317 --> 00:05:33,050
In 36 of 52 liver tumours,

128
00:05:33,050 --> 00:05:35,783
CDK20 was higher than in nearby

129
00:05:35,783 --> 00:05:37,183
non-tumour tissue.

130
00:05:37,217 --> 00:05:39,967
High levels went with later-stage tumours

131
00:05:39,967 --> 00:05:41,417
and shorter survival.

132
00:05:41,417 --> 00:05:45,067
And turning CDK20 down in liver cancer cells

133
00:05:45,067 --> 00:05:46,550
slowed their growth.

134
00:05:47,067 --> 00:05:50,783
So: it is a really strong suspect.

135
00:05:51,767 --> 00:05:55,167
Not the only cause of liver cancer,

136
00:05:55,167 --> 00:05:57,783
but a lock worth trying.

137
00:05:58,233 --> 00:06:00,300
In 2023,

138
00:06:00,300 --> 00:06:02,950
an AI platform called PandaOmics backed

139
00:06:02,950 --> 00:06:04,000
that hunch.

140
00:06:04,233 --> 00:06:06,567
It combed ten liver cancer data sets,

141
00:06:06,567 --> 00:06:08,367
over a thousand tumour samples

142
00:06:08,367 --> 00:06:10,183
and hundreds of healthy ones,

143
00:06:10,183 --> 00:06:11,900
plus research papers.

144
00:06:11,983 --> 00:06:14,467
People checked its top 20 by hand,

145
00:06:14,467 --> 00:06:17,200
and CDK20 came out on top,

146
00:06:17,200 --> 00:06:19,600
with no approved drug against it yet.

147
00:06:19,767 --> 00:06:23,467
And no one had measured CDK20’s shape.

148
00:06:23,650 --> 00:06:26,233
That takes a lot of hard lab work,

149
00:06:26,233 --> 00:06:28,633
and only a small fraction of proteins

150
00:06:28,633 --> 00:06:30,567
have ever been measured.

151
00:06:30,783 --> 00:06:32,317
Without a 3D shape,

152
00:06:32,317 --> 00:06:35,767
even computer predictions of binding get

153
00:06:35,767 --> 00:06:38,733
expensive, slow and difficult.

154
00:06:39,250 --> 00:06:41,833
So you’ve got a lock nobody can see.

155
00:06:42,433 --> 00:06:44,367
How do you cut a key in the dark?

156
00:07:09,917 --> 00:07:11,383
the team did something

157
00:07:11,383 --> 00:07:14,367
that once sounded like science fiction.

158
00:07:14,583 --> 00:07:17,483
They asked an AI to guess the shape.

159
00:07:17,567 --> 00:07:20,833
Imagine a protein as a string of beads

160
00:07:20,833 --> 00:07:23,050
that folds into a 3D shape.

161
00:07:23,133 --> 00:07:25,700
The order of the beads is the sequence.

162
00:07:25,783 --> 00:07:28,550
AlphaFold learned from known structures

163
00:07:28,550 --> 00:07:30,000
how sequences fold.

164
00:07:30,050 --> 00:07:32,783
Give it one, and it predicts the shape,

165
00:07:32,783 --> 00:07:35,950
plus how sure it is about each part.

166
00:07:35,950 --> 00:07:38,833
Before, you’d need a lab-measured structure.

167
00:07:38,917 --> 00:07:39,717
Now?

168
00:07:39,800 --> 00:07:42,483
The team just downloaded a prediction,

169
00:07:42,483 --> 00:07:45,500
for free, from a public database.

170
00:07:45,583 --> 00:07:46,883
According to the paper,

171
00:07:46,883 --> 00:07:49,317
this was the first time AlphaFold

172
00:07:49,317 --> 00:07:52,600
had been used to find a hit like this.

173
00:07:52,700 --> 00:07:53,967
But look closer.

174
00:07:54,050 --> 00:07:55,900
One end of the protein,

175
00:07:55,900 --> 00:07:58,883
a floppy tail, had really low confidence.

176
00:07:58,967 --> 00:08:01,867
And in the model, it lay across the keyhole,

177
00:08:01,867 --> 00:08:03,817
with one building block sitting

178
00:08:03,817 --> 00:08:05,717
inside the ATP pocket.

179
00:08:05,933 --> 00:08:07,117
Trust it blindly,

180
00:08:07,117 --> 00:08:09,433
and you’d cut keys for a blocked door.

181
00:08:09,917 --> 00:08:11,600
So what did they do?

182
00:08:11,800 --> 00:08:13,133
They cut the tail off.

183
00:08:13,133 --> 00:08:14,550
The AI made the guess.

184
00:08:14,800 --> 00:08:16,583
A human caught the problem.

185
00:08:17,433 --> 00:08:20,417
And this next part honestly blew my mind.

186
00:08:20,633 --> 00:08:23,017
Think about how chemists have always found

187
00:08:23,017 --> 00:08:23,850
a key.

188
00:08:23,933 --> 00:08:25,517
Imagine a molecule.

189
00:08:25,550 --> 00:08:26,400
Make it.

190
00:08:26,400 --> 00:08:27,400
Test it.

191
00:08:27,400 --> 00:08:28,467
It fails.

192
00:08:28,700 --> 00:08:29,500
Tweak it.

193
00:08:29,500 --> 00:08:30,667
Try again.

194
00:08:30,917 --> 00:08:33,383
And every single round in a real lab costs

195
00:08:33,383 --> 00:08:34,767
time and money.

196
00:08:34,867 --> 00:08:37,000
Generative AI takes that loop

197
00:08:37,000 --> 00:08:39,133
and moves this into a computer.

198
00:08:39,383 --> 00:08:40,883
The platform here,

199
00:08:40,883 --> 00:08:44,333
Chemistry42, runs dozens of AI models

200
00:08:44,333 --> 00:08:47,533
side by side, built on known chemistry.

201
00:08:47,617 --> 00:08:49,067
They invent new molecules

202
00:08:49,067 --> 00:08:51,000
for this exact keyhole,

203
00:08:51,000 --> 00:08:53,333
score them, learn from the scores,

204
00:08:53,333 --> 00:08:54,550
and go again.

205
00:08:54,850 --> 00:08:57,317
Its makers say it turns out new structures

206
00:08:57,317 --> 00:08:58,683
within hours.

207
00:08:59,200 --> 00:09:01,517
AI didn’t replace the scientists.

208
00:09:01,667 --> 00:09:04,183
It just ran their trial and error

209
00:09:04,183 --> 00:09:05,750
inside a computer,

210
00:09:05,750 --> 00:09:07,017
at light speed.

211
00:09:07,483 --> 00:09:10,033
The team gave it the pocket’s shape

212
00:09:10,033 --> 00:09:11,067
and one rule:

213
00:09:11,067 --> 00:09:14,083
every key must grip a spot called the hinge.

214
00:09:14,300 --> 00:09:17,483
Its filters are built to toss designs

215
00:09:17,483 --> 00:09:20,450
with reactive or possibly toxic groups,

216
00:09:20,450 --> 00:09:22,117
and to score how drug-like

217
00:09:22,117 --> 00:09:24,283
and easy to make each one is.

218
00:09:39,383 --> 00:09:44,083
It came back with 8,918 brand-new designs.

219
00:09:44,200 --> 00:09:45,800
Way too many to make.

220
00:09:45,800 --> 00:09:48,733
So each key gets tried in a virtual lock.

221
00:09:48,950 --> 00:09:50,250
That’s docking:

222
00:09:50,250 --> 00:09:52,217
the computer predicts how a molecule

223
00:09:52,217 --> 00:09:53,933
might sit in the pocket,

224
00:09:53,933 --> 00:09:55,367
and scores the fit.

225
00:09:55,450 --> 00:09:57,533
Similar designs get grouped,

226
00:09:57,533 --> 00:09:59,800
54 make the shortlist,

227
00:09:59,800 --> 00:10:02,167
and chemists pick just seven to make.

228
00:10:02,617 --> 00:10:03,417
Wait.

229
00:10:04,017 --> 00:10:05,967
So the computer picked the winners?

230
00:10:05,967 --> 00:10:07,133
Not quite.

231
00:10:07,133 --> 00:10:09,900
A docking score is a clue,

232
00:10:09,900 --> 00:10:11,083
not proof.

233
00:10:11,217 --> 00:10:12,750
Only the lab can tell.

234
00:10:12,933 --> 00:10:14,883
So, the moment of truth.

235
00:10:15,083 --> 00:10:16,933
Seven real molecules,

236
00:10:16,933 --> 00:10:18,600
made in a real lab.

237
00:10:18,850 --> 00:10:22,100
Do any actually grab CDK20?

238
00:10:22,383 --> 00:10:24,683
Grip is measured as Kd:

239
00:10:24,683 --> 00:10:28,550
roughly, how much molecule it takes to grab

240
00:10:28,550 --> 00:10:30,300
half the protein.

241
00:10:30,300 --> 00:10:31,817
Lower means tighter.

242
00:10:32,300 --> 00:10:34,333
Six options barely held up.

243
00:10:34,333 --> 00:10:36,083
But one option stood out.

244
00:10:36,083 --> 00:10:37,850
Its name is 001.

245
00:10:38,850 --> 00:10:42,833
001’s Kd: 9.2 micromolar.

246
00:10:42,833 --> 00:10:44,067
The other six?

247
00:10:44,217 --> 00:10:45,800
All above 30.

248
00:10:45,800 --> 00:10:46,950
Much weaker.

249
00:10:47,200 --> 00:10:49,000
And here’s the wild part.

250
00:10:49,083 --> 00:10:51,550
The clock read just 30 days,

251
00:10:51,550 --> 00:10:54,333
from picking the target to this first

252
00:10:54,333 --> 00:10:55,350
confirmed hit.

253
00:10:55,450 --> 00:10:57,867
But then came a second test.

254
00:10:57,950 --> 00:11:01,333
Because grabbing a lock isn’t the same

255
00:11:01,333 --> 00:11:02,733
as jamming it.

256
00:11:03,083 --> 00:11:07,483
Does 001 actually stop CDK20 from working?

257
00:11:07,850 --> 00:11:11,100
That’s measured as IC50:

258
00:11:11,100 --> 00:11:13,850
how much you need to cut the enzyme’s

259
00:11:13,850 --> 00:11:15,317
activity in half.

260
00:11:15,400 --> 00:11:16,833
For 001?

261
00:11:16,833 --> 00:11:19,883
Above 6,000 nanomolar.

262
00:11:21,000 --> 00:11:23,067
The key went into the lock.

263
00:11:23,067 --> 00:11:24,983
But it barely turned.

264
00:11:24,983 --> 00:11:27,333
So that’s it?

265
00:11:27,333 --> 00:11:28,700
Back to square one?

266
00:11:29,133 --> 00:11:30,183
Not at all.

267
00:11:30,267 --> 00:11:33,767
This is where the loop kicks in again,

268
00:11:33,767 --> 00:11:36,583
now with real lab data inside.

269
00:11:36,583 --> 00:11:38,367
The results went back in,

270
00:11:38,367 --> 00:11:41,850
with a predicted pose of how 001 sat

271
00:11:41,850 --> 00:11:43,033
in the pocket.

272
00:11:43,200 --> 00:11:44,633
Sixteen new designs.

273
00:11:44,633 --> 00:11:46,350
Six made and tested.

274
00:11:46,450 --> 00:11:49,500
All within 30 days of that first hit.

275
00:11:49,600 --> 00:11:52,167
And one of them, 048,

276
00:11:52,167 --> 00:11:55,000
grabbed about fifteen times tighter.

277
00:11:55,000 --> 00:11:57,633
Then the enzyme test.

278
00:11:57,783 --> 00:11:59,650
Remember, 001 needed

279
00:11:59,650 --> 00:12:03,217
more than 6,000 nanomolar.

280
00:12:03,367 --> 00:12:07,583
048 needed just 33.4.

281
00:12:08,067 --> 00:12:10,833
This time, the key turned.

282
00:12:19,150 --> 00:12:20,867
Then, the big step:

283
00:12:20,867 --> 00:12:22,000
living cells.

284
00:12:22,083 --> 00:12:25,383
After three days with 048,

285
00:12:25,383 --> 00:12:27,633
Huh7 liver cancer cells,

286
00:12:27,633 --> 00:12:29,850
packed with CDK20,

287
00:12:29,850 --> 00:12:33,017
lost half their living-cell signal

288
00:12:33,017 --> 00:12:36,533
at about 210 nanomolar.

289
00:12:36,717 --> 00:12:40,967
Comparison cells, HEK293,

290
00:12:40,967 --> 00:12:44,683
needed about 1,700.

291
00:12:44,700 --> 00:12:46,433
Roughly eight times.

292
00:12:46,683 --> 00:12:48,433
So, the big question.

293
00:12:48,583 --> 00:12:51,567
How much did the computers actually help?

294
00:12:51,700 --> 00:12:53,850
Let’s line up before and now.

295
00:12:53,950 --> 00:12:54,917
Seeing the lock.

296
00:12:54,917 --> 00:12:57,450
Before: hard lab experiments,

297
00:12:57,450 --> 00:12:59,100
and without a 3D shape,

298
00:12:59,100 --> 00:13:00,583
design was expensive,

299
00:13:00,583 --> 00:13:02,100
slow and difficult.

300
00:13:02,117 --> 00:13:03,983
Now: a free prediction,

301
00:13:03,983 --> 00:13:06,433
downloaded from a public database.

302
00:13:06,533 --> 00:13:07,517
Finding a hit.

303
00:13:07,517 --> 00:13:09,183
Before: screening real compounds

304
00:13:09,183 --> 00:13:10,317
by the millions,

305
00:13:10,317 --> 00:13:13,517
an approach a 2023 review says has hit

306
00:13:13,517 --> 00:13:15,167
a costly bottleneck.

307
00:13:15,200 --> 00:13:19,000
Now: 8,918 designs inside a computer,

308
00:13:19,000 --> 00:13:21,350
and just 13 molecules actually made.

309
00:13:21,833 --> 00:13:24,350
Millions of compounds screened before.

310
00:13:24,750 --> 00:13:25,550
Here?

311
00:13:26,250 --> 00:13:27,433
Thirteen made.

312
00:13:27,433 --> 00:13:28,383
Thirteen.

313
00:13:28,900 --> 00:13:30,450
That’s honestly wild.

314
00:13:30,917 --> 00:13:31,717
Time.

315
00:13:31,733 --> 00:13:34,750
Before: a ten-to-seventeen-year road,

316
00:13:34,750 --> 00:13:37,800
with finding a hit just the opening stretch.

317
00:13:37,800 --> 00:13:40,117
Now: 30 days to the first hit,

318
00:13:40,117 --> 00:13:42,383
and a better key within another 30.

319
00:13:42,483 --> 00:13:43,667
Improving the key.

320
00:13:43,667 --> 00:13:46,533
Before: every change meant another costly

321
00:13:46,533 --> 00:13:47,567
lab round.

322
00:13:47,567 --> 00:13:50,283
Now: a predicted picture of the first key

323
00:13:50,283 --> 00:13:52,450
guided sixteen designs,

324
00:13:52,450 --> 00:13:53,433
and the grip got

325
00:13:53,433 --> 00:13:55,483
about fifteen times tighter.

326
00:13:55,583 --> 00:13:56,533
To be fair,

327
00:13:56,533 --> 00:13:58,967
these pairs come from different studies:

328
00:13:58,967 --> 00:14:01,017
they compare scale and speed,

329
00:14:01,017 --> 00:14:03,417
not one race run side by side.

330
00:14:03,550 --> 00:14:06,617
But the paper itself calls the work cost-

331
00:14:06,617 --> 00:14:07,917
and time-efficient.

332
00:14:08,017 --> 00:14:09,567
Fewer failed molecules,

333
00:14:09,567 --> 00:14:12,233
lower costs, fewer animal tests,

334
00:14:12,233 --> 00:14:13,933
less chemical waste.

335
00:14:14,017 --> 00:14:16,267
Computing costs electricity too.

336
00:14:16,350 --> 00:14:17,717
And the real prize?

337
00:14:17,800 --> 00:14:19,083
New treatments for patients.

338
00:14:19,183 --> 00:14:22,383
So where’s 048 on the road?

339
00:14:22,400 --> 00:14:24,150
Target: found.

340
00:14:24,450 --> 00:14:25,967
Hit: found.

341
00:14:25,983 --> 00:14:28,200
Improving it: started.

342
00:14:28,617 --> 00:14:31,233
Cells: first tests, done.

343
00:14:31,250 --> 00:14:33,550
Animals and clinical trials:

344
00:14:33,550 --> 00:14:34,450
not yet.

345
00:14:34,467 --> 00:14:36,083
And here’s what the end of that road

346
00:14:36,083 --> 00:14:37,283
can look like.

347
00:14:37,300 --> 00:14:39,983
Some AI-assisted cancer drugs have walked

348
00:14:39,983 --> 00:14:41,033
much further.

349
00:14:41,050 --> 00:14:43,600
In September 2026,

350
00:14:43,600 --> 00:14:46,367
one of them, lirafugratinib,

351
00:14:46,367 --> 00:14:48,100
was approved in the US

352
00:14:48,100 --> 00:14:49,950
for a bile duct cancer.

353
00:14:49,967 --> 00:14:52,117
Others failed along the way.

354
00:14:52,133 --> 00:14:53,450
And 048?

355
00:14:53,467 --> 00:14:56,683
Its makers chose not to take it to trials,

356
00:14:56,683 --> 00:14:57,517
and shared it

357
00:14:57,517 --> 00:14:59,350
with other researchers instead.

358
00:14:59,833 --> 00:15:02,450
048 is at the very start of the road.

359
00:15:02,600 --> 00:15:04,167
But for this case,

360
00:15:04,167 --> 00:15:05,367
that start took weeks,

361
00:15:05,367 --> 00:15:06,417
not years.

362
00:15:07,600 --> 00:15:09,583
So, what should happen next?

363
00:15:09,883 --> 00:15:12,050
We’d suggest three things.

364
00:15:12,617 --> 00:15:15,417
One: walk 048 further.

365
00:15:15,550 --> 00:15:18,350
It’s public now, so anyone could try.

366
00:15:18,533 --> 00:15:20,833
Test it in more liver cancer cells

367
00:15:20,833 --> 00:15:22,600
and normal liver cells,

368
00:15:22,600 --> 00:15:23,867
then animals,

369
00:15:23,867 --> 00:15:25,917
and check how the body handles it

370
00:15:25,917 --> 00:15:27,483
and what else it hits.

371
00:15:27,683 --> 00:15:30,333
The authors had already listed these steps.

372
00:15:30,433 --> 00:15:32,733
Two: check the AlphaFold model

373
00:15:32,733 --> 00:15:35,133
against a lab-measured structure,

374
00:15:35,133 --> 00:15:36,567
once one exists.

375
00:15:36,733 --> 00:15:38,467
And feed every result,

376
00:15:38,467 --> 00:15:40,183
including the failures,

377
00:15:40,183 --> 00:15:41,850
back into the next round.

378
00:15:41,950 --> 00:15:43,783
Three: race them.

379
00:15:43,917 --> 00:15:46,933
An AI route and a classic route,

380
00:15:46,933 --> 00:15:49,117
same target, same budget,

381
00:15:49,117 --> 00:15:51,017
same definition of a hit.

382
00:15:51,300 --> 00:15:54,000
Then we’d know exactly how much faster AI

383
00:15:54,000 --> 00:15:54,817
really is.

384
00:15:55,317 --> 00:15:57,700
So, back to where we started.

385
00:15:58,150 --> 00:16:00,650
How can computer-aided methods help

386
00:16:00,650 --> 00:16:01,883
scientists find

387
00:16:01,883 --> 00:16:04,383
and assess cancer drug candidates?

388
00:16:04,467 --> 00:16:06,917
In this case, at four points.

389
00:16:06,950 --> 00:16:09,033
Picking a target from data.

390
00:16:09,183 --> 00:16:11,633
Predicting the lock’s shape.

391
00:16:11,850 --> 00:16:13,500
Designing new keys.

392
00:16:13,500 --> 00:16:16,033
And ranking which ones to make.

393
00:16:16,033 --> 00:16:18,733
That’s our first objective,

394
00:16:18,733 --> 00:16:20,350
met for this case.

395
00:16:20,450 --> 00:16:21,833
And our second?

396
00:16:21,833 --> 00:16:24,633
These methods helped the team assess

397
00:16:24,633 --> 00:16:26,250
candidates fast.

398
00:16:26,333 --> 00:16:29,300
Screening by the millions became

399
00:16:29,300 --> 00:16:31,117
thirteen molecules made.

400
00:16:31,200 --> 00:16:33,917
And on a road measured in years,

401
00:16:33,917 --> 00:16:36,183
the first hit came in 30 days,

402
00:16:36,183 --> 00:16:38,183
and round two made the key turn.

403
00:16:38,667 --> 00:16:40,333
This works in cells,

404
00:16:40,333 --> 00:16:42,317
not yet in people.

405
00:16:42,750 --> 00:16:45,083
The computer narrows the search.

406
00:16:45,933 --> 00:16:47,950
Experiments still decide.

407
00:16:48,067 --> 00:16:50,033
So next time you see a headline

408
00:16:50,033 --> 00:16:52,750
like ‘AI discovers a cancer drug’…

409
00:16:52,867 --> 00:16:56,400
…ask where it really is on the road:

410
00:16:56,400 --> 00:16:59,150
computer, protein,

411
00:16:59,150 --> 00:17:03,133
cells, animals, or patients?

412
00:17:03,150 --> 00:17:05,400
AI didn’t replace the scientists.

413
00:17:05,600 --> 00:17:08,667
It gave them trial and error at light speed.

414
00:17:09,083 --> 00:17:10,450
Thanks for watching.

